# Multi-Doc RAG Chatbot (Flask + HTML, zero cost)

A Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) chatbot that lets you upload multiple
documents (PDF, DOCX, TXT) and ask questions about them — built entirely with
free/local tools, no paid database or API required.

## Architecture

```
User uploads PDF/DOCX/TXT
        |
        v
Text extraction (pypdf / python-docx)
        |
        v
Chunking (LangChain RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter)
        |
        v
Embeddings (sentence-transformers, local model, no API cost)
        |
        v
FAISS index (local, in-memory vector store)
        |
        v
User asks a question
        |
        v
Query embedded -> top-k similar chunks retrieved from FAISS
        |
        v
Retrieved chunks + question sent to LLM (Groq free tier)
        |
        v
Answer shown in chat UI (plain HTML/CSS/JS, served by Flask)
```

## Why these choices (talking points for interviews)

- **sentence-transformers (all-MiniLM-L6-v2)** for embeddings instead of
  OpenAI embeddings — runs locally on CPU, zero API cost, good enough quality
  for small-to-medium document sets.
- **FAISS** for the vector store — no hosted DB needed, index lives in memory
  and is rebuilt per session (can be swapped for pgvector/Pinecone later for
  persistence at scale).
- **Groq** for the generation step — has a free tier and serves open models
  (Llama 3.1) fast; swappable with Ollama for a fully offline setup.
- **Flask + plain HTML/CSS/JS** instead of Streamlit — gives full control
  over the UI and demonstrates basic full-stack wiring (REST endpoints +
  fetch calls), which is more relevant if you want to speak to frontend/backend
  separation in an interview.

## Setup

1. Install dependencies:
   ```
   pip install -r requirements.txt
   ```

2. (Optional but recommended) Get a free Groq API key from
   https://console.groq.com and set it as an environment variable:
   ```
   export GROQ_API_KEY="your_key_here"
   ```
   Without this, the app still runs and will show you the raw retrieved
   chunks instead of an LLM-generated answer — useful for testing the
   retrieval step in isolation.

3. Run the app:
   ```
   python app.py
   ```

4. Open your browser at `http://localhost:5000`

## Usage

1. Upload one or more PDF/DOCX/TXT files using the upload box.
2. Wait for the "Processed X file(s)..." confirmation.
3. Ask a question in the chat box — the answer will be grounded in your
   uploaded documents, with source filenames shown below each answer.
4. Use "Clear Knowledge Base" to reset and start with new documents.

## Possible extensions (good to mention as "future work")

- Swap FAISS for pgvector to persist the knowledge base across restarts.
- Add a multi-agent step: one agent for retrieval quality checking, another
  for answer synthesis.
- Add observability: log retrieval latency, chunk relevance scores, and
  flag low-confidence answers.
- Support streaming responses instead of waiting for the full answer.